WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.600
Let’s talk about the protests in Lebanon.
&nbsp;

00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:04.960
People have been demonstrating for months 
&nbsp;

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:06.720
saying they’re tired of corruption 

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:09.040
and a never-ending economic crisis.

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:20.360
For the stop of crushing liberties, freedoms

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:26.400
and to stop the economic nightmare in which we are.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:29.280
In response Prime Minister Saad Hariri resigned

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:31.360
and now Lebanon has a new government.

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:34.160
But protesters are still on the streets.

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:40.280
They say their message is clear: we haven’t given up 

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:42.000
and we aren’t going away.

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:44.520
So why don’t they support the new government? 

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:47.720
And what is the back story for what’s going on in Lebanon?

00:00:55.960 --> 00:00:57.960
Call it an anti-government movement

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:00.040
uprising or even a revolution.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:01.800
What’s going on in Lebanon is something 

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:04.560
many people there say they haven’t seen before.

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:07.680
This is a country with 18 officially recognised  

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:10.600
religious groups, where people have traditionally supported

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:13.280
the interests of their own faith or sect.

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:15.160
But this is a new generation.

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:26.160
People from across the country took to the streets. 

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:28.680
The sectarian divide was no longer there. 

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:30.520
There was no class divide. It was the poor. 

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:32.120
It was the middle class. It was the rich.

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:33.720
People who were just fed up.

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:38.280
Protesters say they’re fed up with a lot of things:

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:40.680
the economy, high taxes, lack of jobs

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:43.920
crumbling infrastructure and they say the biggest problem

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:46.520
is rampant corruption which has thrived 

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:49.440
under Lebanon’s sectarian political system.

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:51.680
We’re going to explain that system in a second

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:53.800
but let’s get a sense of just how tough

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:56.720
everyday life is for most Lebanese people.

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:00.720
We don't really have access to proper public services.

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:02.800
Like there's no 24/7 electricity

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:05.920
there is no clean, accessible drinking water. 

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:08.120
You have piles of garbage all over the country.

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:12.360
Sewage is not treated. It goes straight out to the ocean.

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:14.640
So it feels like the state is absent 

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:17.360
from daily life in Lebanon.

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:19.360
Most people agree that mess is caused by

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:21.440
serious mismanagement.

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:23.640
Some say it’s because of corruption.

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:26.280
But you’ll also hear analysts say that Lebanon never

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:29.360
really recovered from a long and devastating war.

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:39.160
We’re talking about Lebanon’s civil war 

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:43.800
which started in 1975 and went on for 15 years.

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:46.280
Militias from different sects and political groups 

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:47.880
fought each other for dominance 

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:50.600
and also fought among themselves.

00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:52.640
Then Syria and Israel got involved 

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:55.160
and the battles became even more destructive.

00:02:56.760 --> 00:02:59.360
At the end of the war Lebanon’s main political

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:01.400
groups signed the Taif Agreement 

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:03.960
to make sure they all got a slice of political power.

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:06.920
And that’s really the basis for Lebanon’s 

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:09.440
political system today.

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:11.320
A Maronite Christian gets the presidency

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:13.640
the prime minister has to be Sunni Muslim

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:17.480
and the speaker of parliament is always a Shia Muslim.

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:19.560
In parliament seats are shared 50/50  

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:21.720
between Muslims and Christians.

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:24.520
But in reality there are two big political alliances

00:03:24.520 --> 00:03:26.640
which include parties from different sects

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:30.360
and they each have rival foreign powers backing them.

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:33.240
March 14 was former prime minister Saad Hariri’s bloc

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:36.680
which has the support of Saudi Arabia and the US. 

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:38.680
March 8 is the bloc that includes the Maronite 

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:42.520
Christian President Michel Aoun’s party and Hezbollah.

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:45.200
It has Syria and Iran’s support.

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:47.000
So because of all those overlapping

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:50.040
and competing interests many people will tell you that

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:54.480
Lebanon’s sectarian political system isn’t really sectarian.

00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:56.080
What's really happening in Lebanon is that 

00:03:56.080 --> 00:04:00.760
there are political parties that used to be militias.

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:02.680
Sects are not fighting in Lebanon.

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:06.480
It's really opposing warlords backed by
&nbsp;

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:08.680
opposing foreign powers.

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:11.200
And this is how, many say, corruption 

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:13.120
has gotten so out of hand.

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:15.280
Many of those powerful political leaders

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:17.640
or the Zuama as they’re called in Lebanon

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:20.360
are said to have focused on their own political

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:23.920
and financial gains and made themselves very rich.

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:27.800
Lebanon has 220 km of coast. 

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:31.560
180 km of the coast has been privatised.

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:35.840
It's now under the control of either former officials 

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:38.360
and people who are close to them.

00:04:38.360 --> 00:04:41.720
I'll just give you one example. Parking meters in Beirut.

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:44.240
The revenues were not going to the state.

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:47.560
They were going to a private company controlled

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:50.320
owned by people who are close to politicians.

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:52.800
Now that’s not to say that everyone in Lebanon

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:55.560
wants to overhaul the entire system.

00:04:55.560 --> 00:04:58.000
The majority still support the mainly sectarian 

00:04:58.000 --> 00:04:59.880
political parties for a number of reasons. 

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:04.160
One, ideological. Two, they benefit from those in power.

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:06.520
We're talking about at least 400,000 people

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:09.440
employed by the public sector. Now with their families 

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:10.920
could you imagine that number?

00:05:10.920 --> 00:05:14.080
Lebanon’s elite has responded to calls for a clean-up.

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:17.400
In 2019 Prime Minister Saad Hariri stepped down

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:19.720
and after months of talks the new prime minister

00:05:19.720 --> 00:05:21.640
Hassan Diab, who’s a computer engineering

00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:24.960
professor, finally announced his new government.

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:27.160
According to Diab it’s made up of technocrats

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:29.120
with no political loyalties 

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:32.200
and it even has a record number of female ministers.

00:05:32.200 --> 00:05:35.160
But not everyone is convinced this is real change.

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:40.120
The protesters had demanded a completely independent 

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:43.080
technocratic government which just means

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:45.920
they’re politically independent and also

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:48.200
that they are experts in their fields. 

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:50.680
It's kind of just the same thing. Like different faces

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:53.920
but same parties, same alliances. 

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:55.960
Critics of the new government are saying that

00:05:55.960 --> 00:05:58.080
many of those technocrats have the support

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:01.840
of the March 8 alliance, the one that includes Hezbollah.

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:03.480
And that complicates things.

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:08.160
Hezbollah is a Shia Muslim political group 

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:11.320
and arguably Lebanon’s most powerful player.

00:06:11.320 --> 00:06:15.280
It has a military wing the size of a small army funded by Iran.

00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:17.720
Hezbollah is considered a 

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:19.960
terrorist organisation by the US. 

00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:23.120
And the EU has blacklisted its armed wing.

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:25.800
Even just the perception of Hezbollah pulling the strings

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:28.160
might make it hard for Lebanon’s new government

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:31.640
to get the international funding it desperately needs.

00:06:31.640 --> 00:06:34.240
This is a country with the world’s third highest level of 

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:36.640
debt when you compare it to its small economy.

00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:39.400
Its debt is more than $80 billion.

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:42.160
Lebanon’s former finance minister recently told Reuters

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:45.080
news agency that the country will need at least

00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:48.760
a $20 billion bailout, possibly from the IMF

00:06:48.760 --> 00:06:51.120
World Bank and Gulf countries.

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:54.600
In 2018 international donors agreed to give Lebanon

00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:58.200
$11 billion in loans, grants and donations 

00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:00.720
but Lebanon hasn’t seen that money yet.

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:03.800
The message that Beirut is getting is enough is enough

00:07:03.800 --> 00:07:08.640
you need to undertake painful but necessary reforms

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:11.120
in order for this funding to be released.

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:13.320
People in Lebanon are already feeling

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:14.920
the crisis getting worse.

00:07:14.920 --> 00:07:17.280
Since protests began the value of Lebanon’s currency 

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:20.760
has fallen by 60% and banks are already

00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:24.280
limiting cash withdrawals to about $200 a week. 

00:07:24.280 --> 00:07:28.240
Violence is also escalating as police try to contain the rage.

00:07:33.320 --> 00:07:36.240
So there’s defiance and some hope among protesters

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:38.920
that their campaign will lead somewhere.

00:07:38.920 --> 00:07:40.680
But other Lebanese people we spoke to

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:42.840
say there’s still a very long road ahead.

00:07:42.840 --> 00:07:45.040
One of our Lebanese colleagues despaired

00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:47.560
that war-torn Syria might be back on its feet

00:07:47.560 --> 00:07:49.960
before things get better in Lebanon.

